    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. that's a good start. could you expand a bit more on the specific risks of vertical scaling? like, if we keep pushing the limits of a single instance to handle spikes, what kind of failure modes are we actually looking at from a system stability standpoint?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Title: Risks and Limitations of Vertical Scaling on AWS for Microservices\n\nSummary: While vertical scaling involves adding more power to a single server, which can initially address traffic spikes, it comes with significant downsides as it pushes hardware limits. Hardware failures, overheating, and poor performance under heavy loads become risks. Additionally, resource capacity is the single dominant constraint, meaning subsequent scaling requires purchasing new, more powerful hardware. This not only escalates costs but also extends the time to adapt to changing demand patterns, potentially leading to downtime or degraded service levels if not managed with precision. With microservices, over-reliance on vertical scaling can also lead to deployment blockers, as all services must support the upgraded specs of the singular, now costly, server infrastructure.\n\nAction Items:\n- Rigorously monitor server health and performance metrics during high-load scenarios to prevent points of failure.\n- Institute a comprehensive backup and disaster recovery plan that factors in the potential for single point of failure.\n- Consider adopting an auto-scaling strategy that primarily favors horizontal scaling to maintain service stability and scalability.\n- Invest in continuous performance testing to identify and address bottlenecks before they become critical under peak loads.",
      "source": "target",
